messages:
Maria G: Hey team — running into a funky edge with streaming funcalls + structured outputs. When the model streams a funcall payload in pieces, the SDK's TS parser throws because the partial chunks aren't valid JSON. Example stream chunk sequence in logs: 
```
chunk1: '{"tool":"order.create","args":{"item":"coffee",'
chunk2: '"qty":1, "metadata":{"notes":"decaf"}}}
'
```
The SDK tries to JSON.parse per 'data:' event and fails. Any lightweight helper patterns people use?

AJ: We've hit this before. Two common options: 1) buffer until you see a full top-level brace match (naive), 2) a small streaming-json-framer that emits when a parse succeeds. I prefer option 2 because of nested braces.

Sam K: +1 to framer. Also watch out for auth refresh killing the SSE/fetch stream mid-buffer — that can leave partial JSON forever. We add an idempotency token on the funcall so retries are safe and then re-open the stream on a fresh token.

Maria G: Good call on idempotency. For the framer approach, any example helper we could put in the TS SDK? Something like `accumulateUntilValidJSON`?

lucy-sdk: I prototyped a tiny helper in our internal SDK last week. It basically accumulates bytes and attempts parse only when a top-level object close appears (tracking depth, ignoring strings/escapes). Pseudocode we used: 
```
function streamJsonObjects(stream) {
  let buf = ''
  let depth = 0
  let inString = false
  let esc = false
  for await (const chunk of stream) {
    for (const ch of chunk) {
      buf += ch
      if (ch === '"' && !esc) inString = !inString
      if (!inString) {
        if (ch === '{') depth++
        if (ch === '}') depth--
      }
      esc = (ch === '\' && !esc)
    }
    if (depth === 0 && buf.trim()) {
      try {
        yield JSON.parse(buf)
      } catch (e) {
        // fallback: try incremental trims or log + drop
        console.warn('parse failed, dropping buffer', buf.slice(0,80))
      }
      buf = ''
    }
  }
}
```
That handled most fragmented funcall payloads for us.

AJ: Nice. Couple of caveats: 1) streaming arrays or multiple top-level objects in one stream event need to be split, 2) don't block forever on unclosed braces — expose a timeout or emission heuristic.

Maria G: Good note. We also need to maintain typing in TS so the user gets the typed FuncCall object. How do we coerce optional/missing fields without losing type safety?

lucy-sdk: We keep two layers: rawAny from the stream (unknown) then a small validator/coercer using zod-lite (we use tiny custom validator to avoid dependency bloat). Example: 
```
const FuncCallSchema = z.object({
  tool: z.string(),
  args: z.record(z.any()).optional(),
})
const vuln = FuncCallSchema.safeParse(rawAny)
if (!vuln.success) {
  // try to coerce common cases: args as stringified JSON
  if (typeof rawAny.args === 'string') rawAny.args = safeJsonParse(rawAny.args)
}
return FuncCallSchema.parse(rawAny)
```
This preserves static types for callers (`as FuncCall`).

Sam K: Also worth noting: sometimes the model emits `args: null` vs `args: {}`. We normalize `null -> undefined` before coercion so consumers don't hit `cannot read property` bugs.

Maria G: Awesome. One more thing — retries. If a request fails and we retry, the server might re-stream the same funcall. How do we prevent duplicate side effects?

AJ: Use an idempotency token per user-action and pass `X-Idempotency-Key` in request headers (or part of the funcall metadata). Server should dedupe based on that. For the SDK, expose a small helper `withIdempotency(key, fn)` that auto-generates and attaches the key.

lucy-sdk: We've got a small snippet if anyone wants to steal it: 
```
async function callWithIdempotency(client, payload, opts={}){
  const key = opts.key ?? `sdk-${Date.now()}-${Math.random().toString(36).slice(2,8)}`
  return client.post('/v1/funcall', payload, {headers: {'x-idempotency-key': key}})
}
```
PR link: <https://git.internal/redwood/sdk/pull/412> (draft)

Maria G: Perfect. Can we add an example to the TS SDK docs showing: streaming framer + coercer + idempotency header + reconnect-on-refresh? I'll draft a short example tomorrow and link the PR.

devex-bot: Reminder: include a unit test for partial-chunk parsing + an integration test that simulates token refresh mid-stream. See templates at docs/playbook/testing.md :eyes: 

Sam K: I'll add the unit test. Quick Q: should the framer live in core `stream-utils` or under `funcall`?

lucy-sdk: core `stream-utils` — other features (embeddings streaming, conv streaming) can reuse it.

Maria G: Cool. I'll open a PR with a minimal helper + docs. Assigning @lucy-sdk and @Sam K for review. Thanks team :rocket:
